# Starting the Laboratory

Clone the repository:

```powershell
git clone https://github.com/mohammad-darvish-pixel/ICMP-Tunnel-Lab.git
```

Enter the directory:

```powershell
cd ICMP-Tunnel-Lab
```

Then run:

```powershell
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\launcher.ps1
```

You can also use the included:

```text
start_lab.bat
```

The batch launcher starts `launcher.ps1` with an execution-policy bypass. If UAC blocks the process, run the batch file as Administrator.

---

# Running Manually

If you do not want to use the launcher, the components can be started manually.

Start the receiver:

```powershell
python Receiver.py
```

Then start the sender:

```powershell
python sender.py
```

The sender will ask for:

```text
Target IP
File to exfiltrate
```

The default values are:

```text
Target IP:
127.0.0.1

File:
secret.txt
```

The sender also accepts command-line arguments:

```powershell
python sender.py 127.0.0.1 secret.txt
```

The sender implementation uses Scapy to construct the ICMP packets and sends them sequentially with a short delay between packets.

